      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "To determine the type and frequency of emergencies in general practice, and the extent to which general practices are equipped to appropriately respond to emergencies."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "DESIGN",
          "content": "Random-sample, cross-sectional questionnaire survey of general practitioners, October 1999 - March 2000."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "SETTING",
          "content": "General practices in south-east Queensland."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "PARTICIPANTS",
          "content": "512 of 900 eligible GPs in current clinical practice."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "MAIN OUTCOME MEASURES",
          "content": "The type and frequency of medical emergencies presenting to GPs, and descriptive details of emergency drugs and equipment available in their practices."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "512 GPs (response rate, 57%) reported managing a cumulative total of 5640 emergencies over the preceding 12 months. Non-metropolitan GPs saw about 30% more emergencies than their metropolitan counterparts (median, 9 and 7, respectively; P=0.02). The most common emergencies (seen by more than 30% of all GPs) were acute asthma, psychiatric emergencies, convulsions, hypoglycaemia, anaphylaxis, impaired consciousness, shock, poisoning and overdose. Most GPs (77%) stocked 15 or more of the 16 emergency doctor's bag drugs, but a smaller proportion (67%) had all of the basic emergency equipment items considered essential."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "A substantial number of patients with potentially life-threatening emergencies present to GPs. Doctor's bag emergency drugs are available in most general practices, but availability of basic emergency equipment is suboptimal."
        }
      ]},
